<title>USA: U.S. dismayed by EU shipyard aid proposal.</title>
<headline>U.S. dismayed by EU shipyard aid proposal.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky said Friday she was dismayed by a European Union proposal to provide some $2.1 billion in subsidies for shipyards in Spain, Germany and Greece.</p>
<p>The United States has formally requested an explanation of the proposed subsidy from the EU Commission and how it comports with their commitments under an Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development shipbuilding agreement calling for an end to subsidies.</p>
<p>&quot;The EU and all signatory parties to the OECD Shipbuilding Agreement have clear obligations they must adhere to under this accord,&quot; Barshefsky said.</p>
<p>&quot;We are seeking an immediate explanation given the EU's commitments under the agreement.&quot;</p>
<p>The effective date of the OECD agreement, originally scheduled for January 1996, was delayed by the failure of the U.S. Congress to ratify it. The USTR said all other signatories -- the EU, Japan, South Korea and Norway -- have ratified the accord.</p>
<p>Barshefsky said the administration remained committed to winning Congressional approval and would present a revised legislative package to Congress in the near future.</p>
</text>
